# 8. Extra findings that directly constrain a read-receipt implementation

**a. The upstream event shape** — `/Users/ryanzhu/Projects/spectrum-ts/node_modules/@photon-ai/advanced-imessage/dist/groups-CYV3GKtn.d.ts:636-641` (plus `EventContext` at `:582-587`):

```ts
interface EventContext {
    readonly actor?: SingleServiceAddressInfo;
    readonly chatGuid: string;
    readonly isFromMe: boolean;
    readonly occurredAt: Date;
}
…
| (EventContext & {
    readonly type: "message.read";
    readonly sequence: number;
    readonly messageGuid: string;
    readonly readAt: Date;
})
```

So you get: reader = `event.actor` (optional!), chat = `event.chatGuid`, target = `event.messageGuid` (no `targetPartIndex`), two timestamps (`readAt` vs `occurredAt`), `sequence`, `isFromMe`. There is a separate `ChatEvent` variant `chat.markedRead` (`:594-596`) on the `chats` stream, which is not subscribed at all today.

**b. `isFromMe` ambiguity is the single biggest design risk.** For `message.received` it means "I authored the message"; for `message.read` the receipt is *about* one of your outbound messages, so `isFromMe` plausibly reflects the underlying message, not the reader. If it's `true` for peer-read-your-message, `isEventFromCurrentAccount(event, phone)` (`stream.ts:56-63`) would suppress 100% of useful receipts. The `actor`-based half of the guard (`actor.address === phone`) is the part you actually want (suppress *your own* `chats.markRead()` echo). Plan should either (i) use an actor-only self-check, or (ii) verify empirically. `SHARED_PHONE` lines can't do the actor comparison at all (`phone !== SHARED_PHONE` short-circuit).

**c. Core `read` content is outbound-shaped and its target is NOT wrapped.** `/Users/ryanzhu/Projects/spectrum-ts/packages/core/src/content/read.ts:28-33`:

```ts
export const readSchema = z.object({
  type: z.literal("read"),
  target: z.custom<Message>(isMessage, {
    message: "read target must be a Message",
  }),
});
```
`isMessage` only checks `"id" in v && "content" in v`, so a raw provider record parses fine (same trick reactions use). But `wrapNestedContent` in `/Users/ryanzhu/Projects/spectrum-ts/packages/core/src/platform/build.ts:536-598` handles **only** `reaction`, `reply`, `edit`, and `group` — **there is no `read` branch**, so a `read` target emitted from the stream would reach the app as an unwrapped raw record (no `.react()`, `.reply()`, `.edit()`, no `space`/`platform`). Adding an inbound read receipt that carries a real target almost certainly needs a `content.type === "read"` branch in `wrapNestedContent` wrapping the target as `"outbound"` (the read message is one of ours) — mirroring the `edit` branch's reasoning at `build.ts:570-584`.

Also note `readSchema` is deliberately excluded from `baseContentSchemas` (`packages/core/src/content/types.ts:42-63`, comment at `:35-41`) and only appears in the full `contentSchema` (`:72-78`) — so `read` can never be nested inside a reply/edit, which is fine for a top-level inbound message.

**d. `read()` the builder throws on non-inbound targets** (`packages/core/src/content/read.ts:50-61`) — that's the outbound path only; it doesn't constrain a provider constructing `asRead({ target })` directly, but any docs/example must not suggest re-sending an inbound read.

**e. Nothing in the repo currently produces an inbound read receipt** for any provider: Telegram (`packages/telegram/src/outbound/send.ts:170-173`) and Slack (`packages/slack/src/messages.ts:311-313`) no-op on outbound read; there is zero inbound handling. The `events?` escape hatch exists as an alternative surface (`packages/core/src/platform/types.ts:347-359` explicitly names "read receipts" as the motivating example — `spectrum.presence`, `slack.readReceipt`), so a plan could choose a dedicated `events: { readReceipt: ... }` channel instead of the `messages` stream. That would avoid the `wrapNestedContent` gap entirely but wouldn't reach `app.messages`.

**f. `message.unsent`, `message.edited`, `message.reactionRemoved`, `message.stickerPlaced`** all currently fall into the same catch-all at `stream.ts:156-160` — whatever branch structure you add for `message.read` sets the precedent for those.

**g. Read receipts are high-volume.** The dedupe primitives that exist: `MessageCache` (FIFO 1000, `cache.ts:19-47`) and `ContactShareTracker`'s `LRUCache` with TTL (`remote/contact-share.ts:35-46`, `max: 10_000`, `ttl: 24h`, `ttlAutopurge: false`) — the latter is the pattern to copy if per-chat/per-message receipt coalescing is wanted. Stream-level dedupe already exists per-cursor via `deliveredSinceCursor` in `packages/core/src/utils/resumable-stream.ts:202`, `:248-264`, keyed on `ResumableStreamItem.id`.

