toolUseResult.content:
# Inbound read receipts — iMessage (ENG-2101)

## Context

When a recipient reads a message the agent sent, `@photon-ai/advanced-imessage` delivers a `message.read` event on the stream we already subscribe to — and we drop it on the floor. `toMessageItem` (`packages/imessage/src/remote/stream.ts:113-161`) converts only `message.received` and `message.reactionAdded`; everything else hits a terminal `return { cursor, id, values: [] }`. The cursor advances, nothing is emitted.

Today `read` is outbound-only: `space.send(read(msg))` marks *their* messages read. This adds the inverse — **the other side read the message we sent** — as an inbound `Message` on `app.messages`.

Outcome: a dev can tell when, and by whom, each of their outbound messages was read, with per-reader attribution in group chats.

Out of scope, deliberately: `chat.markedRead` on the `chats` stream is documented in `chat_types.proto:62` as "Chat unread count transitioned to zero (locally or via continuity)" — that's *our own* unread count, an echo of our own `markRead`, not a peer receipt. We do not subscribe `chats.subscribeEvents()`. WhatsApp `statuses[].status === "read"` is a separate issue (the provider ignores the `statuses` webhook branch entirely today).

---

## What a consumer dev writes

```ts
for await (const [space, message] of app.messages) {
  switch (message.content.type) {
    case "text":
      await message.reply(`echo: ${message.content.text}`);
      break;

    case "read": {
      // Someone read a message we sent. `read` is one content type used in
      // both directions — same house rule as `reaction` and the membership
      // types. `direction` is the guard.
      if (message.direction !== "inbound") break;

      const target = message.content.target; // the message WE sent
      console.log(
        `${message.sender?.id} read "${target.id}" at ${message.timestamp.toISOString()}`
      );
      break;
    }
  }
}
```

The exact shapes:

```ts
message.direction            // "inbound"
message.sender               // User | undefined — the READER. Always set (§ Absent actor)
message.timestamp            // Date — event.readAt: when the reader's device marked it read
message.space                // Space — same object as the tuple's `space`

message.content              // narrowed to Read by `case "read":`
// export interface Read { type: "read"; target: Message }

message.content.target            // Message — fully built, not a stub
message.content.target.id         // the guid of the message the agent sent
message.content.target.direction  // "outbound"
message.content.target.content    // the text/attachment/etc. we sent
message.content.target.edit(…)    // available — target is built with outbound affordances
message.content.target.unsend()
```

iMessage-narrowed reader identity:

```ts
const im = imessage(message);
im.sender?.address;  // "+15551234567"
im.sender?.service;  // "iMessage" | "SMS" | "RCS" | "unknown"
```

Group chats emit **one message per reader**, sharing `content.target.id`. Aggregating "everyone has read it":

```ts
const readers = new Map<string, Set<string>>(); // target id -> reader ids

case "read": {
  if (message.direction !== "inbound") break;
  const set = readers.get(message.content.target.id) ?? new Set();
  set.add(message.sender?.id ?? "");
  readers.set(message.content.target.id, set);

  const members = await space.getMembers();
  if (set.size >= members.length - 1) {
    console.log(`everyone read ${message.content.target.id}`);
  }
  break;
}
```

**Invariant to document loudly:** `message.sender` is the reader; `message.content.target` is ours. Never the other way round.

---

## Design decisions

**1. Reuse the `read` content type bidirectionally.** No new content arm, no dotted taxonomy. This is the stated house rule (`packages/core/src/content/types.ts:28-33`: "Content values describe state changes and are direction-agnostic… The Message envelope carries `direction` and the actor") and the precedent set by ENG-1878 for inbound group events. `readSchema` is already in the `contentSchema` union (`types.ts:77`) — no union change needed, and existing exhaustive `switch` statements already compile.

The `read()` *builder*'s `direction !== "inbound"` throw (`content/read.ts:53-57`) is an outbound-API guard and **stays**. Inbound receipts never go through the builder — providers call `readSchema.parse` directly, the same way `reactions.ts:52` does with `reactionSchema`.

**2. On by default, no config key.** Per your call. Consequence to accept knowingly: existing `for await` loops start seeing a content type they've never received, and volume is one event per participant per message read — a peer opening a chat with 5 unread agent messages in a 10-person group can emit up to 50 messages. The two filters below are what bound it; the docs must call it out.

**3. Never trust `event.isFromMe`.** `MessageChangeEvent.is_from_me` has no proto comment and most plausibly describes the *underlying message* — which for a genuine receipt is ours, i.e. `true`. Passing a read event to the existing `isEventFromCurrentAccount` (`stream.ts:56-63`) would therefore suppress 100% of useful receipts. Self-suppression instead comes from two checks that don't depend on it:

- cheap pre-filter: `actor.address === phone` (a dedicated line reading in its own Messages app);
- load-bearing: **the resolved target must be `direction: "outbound"`**. A peer's receipt always points at a message we sent. That single check also suppresses the echo of our own `chats.markRead()`, which marks *their* inbound messages and resolves `direction: "inbound"`.

**4. Reuse, don't duplicate.** Extract the cache→fetch→rebuild resolver currently private in `reactions.ts:58-85` instead of copy-pasting it.

---

## Implementation

### Core (3 files)

**`packages/core/src/authoring.ts:53`** — export the schema so providers can parse a raw record. `asRead` is typed `(input: { target: Message })` and won't accept a `ProviderMessageRecord`. Matches `reactionSchema` (`:52`), `replySchema` (`:55`), `groupSchema` (`:44`).

```ts
export { asRead, readSchema } from "./content/read";
```

**`packages/core/src/platform/build.ts`** — add a `read` arm to `wrapNestedContent`, between the `edit` arm (ends `:584`) and the `group` arm (`:585`). Without it `message.content.target` reaches the app as a raw record: it *looks* plausible (it carries `space`, `timestamp`, `direction`) but silently lacks `.reply()`, `.react()`, `.edit()`, `.unsend()`, and `.platform`. A type lie, not an obvious absence.

```ts
  if (content.type === "read") {
    const target = content.target as unknown;
    if (isRawProviderRecord(target)) {
      // A read receipt's target is always one of *our* messages — the one the
      // reader just read — so wrap it outbound, same as `edit`. Providers that
      // resolved the real target set `target.direction` and
      // `wrapProviderMessage` honors it via `rawDirection`; the literal here is
      // the fallback for providers that only have a stub.
      //
      // Inert on the outbound path: `read(message)` always targets an
      // already-built Message, which `isRawProviderRecord` rejects.
      return {
        ...content,
        target: wrapProviderMessage(target, ctx, "outbound"),
      };
    }
    return content;
  }
```

**`packages/core/src/webhook/deserialize.ts`** — add `case "read":` to `mapContent` (`:125-174`, after `case "reply":`) plus a `deserializeRead` beside `deserializeReaction` (`:270-279`). Without it an inbound `read` over the native webhook degrades to `{ type: "custom" }` and the consumer's `case "read":` never fires.

```ts
// An inbound read receipt. The reader rides on the envelope
// (`message.sender`); `target` is the message of *ours* that was read. Targets
// arrive content-less over the wire, so `buildTargetRecord` substitutes the
// 80-char `contentPreview`. `direction: "outbound"` marks the target as ours.
const deserializeRead = (
  raw: Record<string, unknown>,
  spaceRef: SpaceRef
): Content =>
  ({
    type: "read",
    target: {
      ...buildTargetRecord(raw.target, spaceRef),
      direction: "outbound" as const,
    },
  }) as unknown as Content;
```

This is a dead path until Cloud relays inbound reads, but it's the piece that lets any future provider light up with no second API decision. Reuses `buildTargetRecord` (`:295-309`).

No change to `content/types.ts`, `content/read.ts`, or `FIRE_AND_FORGET_TYPES` (already contains `"read"`, `build.ts:66`).

### iMessage (3 files)

**`packages/imessage/src/remote/inbound.ts`** — extract the shared resolver. Place it after `cacheMessage` (ends `:459`) and directly above `getMessage` (`:485-542`), so the "drops vs. rethrows" distinction between the two is visible. Every import it needs is already in the file.

```ts
/**
 * Resolve a guid to the spectrum message it maps to, for events that reference
 * their target only by guid (reactions, read receipts). Cache first — outbound
 * sends are cached at send time by `cacheRemoteOutbound`, inbound messages when
 * they are converted — then one `messages.get` + rebuild, which also warms the
 * cache so sibling events for the same guid (a group's other participants) are
 * free.
 *
 * Any failure drops the event: these are secondary signals and must never wedge
 * the stream. Deliberately unlike `getMessage` below (the public
 * `space.getMessage` action), which rethrows non-`NotFoundError` failures, and
 * unlike it this never resolves `p:N/guid` child ids — event payloads always
 * carry the parent guid.
 */
export const resolveTargetMessage = async (
  client: AdvancedIMessage,
  cache: MessageCache,
  chatGuid: string,
  targetGuid: string,
  phone: string
): Promise<IMessageMessage | undefined> => {
  const cached = cache.get(targetGuid);
  if (cached) {
    return cached;
  }
  try {
    const fetched = await client.messages.get(toMessageGuid(targetGuid));
    const rebuilt = await rebuildFromAppleMessage(
      client,
      fetched,
      phone,
      chatGuid
    );
    cacheMessage(cache, rebuilt);
    return rebuilt;
  } catch {
    return;
  }
};
```

**`packages/imessage/src/remote/reactions.ts:58-85`** — delegate to it. Behavior is bit-for-bit identical (the original passed no `cache`/`visitedReplyGuids` to `rebuildFromAppleMessage` either). Part-index drilling stays here; read receipts have no `targetPartIndex`.

```ts
const resolveReactionTarget = async (
  client: AdvancedIMessage,
  cache: MessageCache,
  chat: string,
  targetGuid: string,
  partIndex: number | undefined,
  phone: string
): Promise<IMessageMessage | undefined> => {
  const candidate = await resolveTargetMessage(
    client,
    cache,
    chat,
    targetGuid,
    phone
  );
  if (!candidate) {
    return;
  }
  // Tapbacks address an ordered part inside a multi-part message; read
  // receipts have no `targetPartIndex`, so part drilling stays here rather
  // than in the shared resolver.
  if (candidate.content.type === "group") {
    const items = candidate.content.items;
    if (!Array.isArray(items)) {
      return candidate;
    }
    const item = items[partIndex ?? 0];
    return isIMessageMessage(item) ? item : candidate;
  }
  return candidate;
};
```

Import change at `:14-19`: add `resolveTargetMessage`, drop the now-unused `cacheMessage` and `rebuildFromAppleMessage`.

**`packages/imessage/src/remote/read-receipts.ts`** — new module, structured like `group-events.ts` (exported id helper + mapper).

```ts
type ReadEvent = Extract<MessageEvent, { type: "message.read" }>;

/**
 * Synthetic id for a `message.read` event — shared between the stream item (the
 * dedup key across live/catch-up) and the surfaced message. `sequence` is
 * monotonic per line, so N participants reading the same message produce N
 * distinct ids, and the same event replayed through catch-up produces the id it
 * produced live.
 */
export const readReceiptMessageId = (event: ReadEvent): string =>
  `${event.messageGuid}:read:${event.sequence}`;

const asProviderRead = (target: IMessageMessage): Read =>
  readSchema.parse({ target, type: "read" });

/**
 * Convert a `message.read` event into an inbound `read` message.
 *
 * Two guards, cheapest first:
 *
 * 1. **No actor -> drop.** Unlike a membership change (where the state change
 *    itself is the payload and `sender: undefined` still carries meaning), the
 *    reader's identity *is* the payload of a receipt. A senderless receipt in a
 *    group would corrupt any "N of M have read" tally. Same call as
 *    `toReactionMessages`.
 *
 * 2. **The target must be one of ours.** `event.isFromMe` is not trustworthy
 *    here: the proto carries no comment for it on this arm, and it most
 *    plausibly describes the *underlying message* — which for a genuine receipt
 *    is ours, i.e. `true` — so keying self-suppression off it would suppress
 *    every receipt worth surfacing. The durable invariant is that a peer's
 *    receipt always points at a message we sent, so the resolved target must be
 *    `direction: "outbound"`. That also suppresses the echo of our own
 *    `chats.markRead()`, which marks *their* inbound messages.
 */
export const toReadReceiptMessages = async (
  client: AdvancedIMessage,
  cache: MessageCache,
  event: ReadEvent,
  phone: string
): Promise<IMessageMessage[]> => {
  if (!event.actor?.address) {
    return [];
  }

  const target = await resolveTargetMessage(
    client,
    cache,
    event.chatGuid,
    event.messageGuid,
    phone
  );
  if (target?.direction !== "outbound") {
    return [];
  }

  return [
    {
      // No `direction` on the envelope: the stream path defaults provider
      // records to inbound. The *target* keeps its own `direction: "outbound"`,
      // which `wrapProviderMessage` honors via `rawDirection`.
      id: readReceiptMessageId(event),
      content: asProviderRead(target),
      sender: toSenderRef(event.actor),
      space: {
        id: event.chatGuid,
        type: chatTypeFromGuid(event.chatGuid),
        phone,
      },
      // `readAt` is when the reader's device marked it read; `occurredAt` is
      // when the bridge observed it. Prefer the former — the entire value of a
      // receipt is *when*. The `??` guards an unset proto timestamp decoding to
      // `undefined` despite the non-optional TS type, the same defensive shape
      // as `dateCreated` at inbound.ts:437.
      timestamp: event.readAt ?? event.occurredAt,
    },
  ];
};
```

**`packages/imessage/src/remote/stream.ts`** — two changes.

(a) Split the actor half out of `isEventFromCurrentAccount` (`:56-63`) so it's usable without `isFromMe`. No behavior change for the three existing callers (`:140`, `:171`, `:197`).

```ts
// The acting party's identity, without the `isFromMe` half. Split out because
// `message.read`'s `isFromMe` is undocumented and most plausibly describes the
// *underlying message* — ours, for a genuine receipt — so trusting it there
// would suppress every receipt worth surfacing.
const isActorCurrentAccount = (
  actor: SingleServiceAddressInfo | undefined,
  phone: string
): boolean =>
  phone !== SHARED_PHONE &&
  actor?.address !== undefined &&
  actor.address === phone;

const isEventFromCurrentAccount = (
  event: Pick<MessageEvent | PollEvent | GroupEvent, "actor" | "isFromMe">,
  phone: string
): boolean => event.isFromMe || isActorCurrentAccount(event.actor, phone);
```

Add `type SingleServiceAddressInfo` to the `@photon-ai/advanced-imessage/grpc` import (`:1-8`).

(b) Add the branch to `toMessageItem`, after the `message.reactionAdded` block (`:154`) and before the catch-all (`:156`). No signature change — the mapper is always on.

```ts
  if (event.type === "message.read") {
    const id = readReceiptMessageId(event);
    // Deliberately `isActorCurrentAccount`, not `isEventFromCurrentAccount`:
    // `isFromMe` is untrustworthy on this arm. This only catches a dedicated
    // line reading in its own Messages app; the load-bearing check is in
    // `toReadReceiptMessages`, which requires the target to be one of ours.
    if (isActorCurrentAccount(event.actor, phone)) {
      return { cursor, id, values: [] };
    }

    const cache = getMessageCache(client);
    return {
      cursor,
      id,
      values: await toReadReceiptMessages(client, cache, event, phone),
    };
  }
```

Nothing else in the stream changes. `isMessageEvent` is prefix-based (`:221-222`) so catch-up replay already includes `message.read`; `streamLabel`'s `"messages" | "polls" | "groups"` union is untouched — receipts ride the existing `messages` stream; `clientStream`/`messages` need no new parameters.

**No change** to `packages/imessage/src/types.ts` (no config flag, and no `readAt` extra — `timestamp` *is* `readAt`, so an extra would be duplication, and every field added to `messageSchema` is permanent public API), `remote/api.ts`, or `index.ts`.

---

## Engineering notes

**Ordering and dedup.** `id: ${messageGuid}:read:${sequence}` is derived purely from the event, so catch-up replay produces the id it produced live — which is what `deliveredSinceCursor.has(item.id)` (`packages/core/src/utils/resumable-stream.ts:253`) needs. `sequence` is monotonic per line, so N readers get N distinct ids, and the `read` namespace can't collide with `${guid}:reaction:${sequence}`. Changing away from the current catch-all id is safe: `item.id` is never persisted — it lives only in the in-memory `Set` at `:202`, cleared on cursor advance. Only the cursor (`String(event.sequence)`) survives a reconnect, and that's untouched.

**Cache misses cost one RPC, on the critical path.** `resumable-stream.ts:384-385` awaits `processLive` serially, so the fetch head-of-line-blocks real inbound messages on that line. Amplification is smaller than it looks: `resolveTargetMessage` calls `cacheMessage` on success, so in a 10-person group reader #1 pays one RPC and warms the cache; #2–#10 are free. Amplification is O(distinct messages read), not O(messages × readers). Combined with `cacheRemoteOutbound` (`index.ts:112-131`) caching every send, steady-state hit rate is near 100%.

The gap is cold start: `stream.ts:121-123` early-returns self-sent `message.received` events *before* caching, so our outbound messages enter the cache only via `cacheRemoteOutbound`. After a restart, catch-up replays receipts for pre-restart sends and every one misses; those that can't be fetched are dropped. Accepted for v1 — cache-only would mean "receipts silently stop working after every deploy." Optional Phase-5 hardening if telemetry warrants: a bounded `Set<string>` of guids that failed to resolve, keyed by client through a `WeakMap` like `getMessageCache` (`cache.ts:85-100`), so a purged message doesn't re-pay a failing RPC per reader.

Pre-existing wart, not fixed here: the bare `catch { return; }` swallows `retryable` errors so they never reach `skipUnmappable`'s rethrow (`stream.ts:89-91`). That's today's reaction behavior; changing it belongs in its own PR.

**Timestamp is `readAt`.** `occurredAt` is when the bridge observed the event; these diverge materially when a receipt arrives via Continuity sync, and using the less-precise field defeats the whole feature.

**Groups emit N messages, one per reader.** That's the only shape that answers who read it. Coalescing would need buffering plus an arbitrary debounce, would have to guess when the reader set is complete, and would destroy per-reader timestamps. Consumers aggregate by `content.target.id` — see the snippet above. This is the number-one thing that will surprise people; it must be prominent in the docs.

**Absent actor → drop**, matching reactions. `group-events.ts:129-135` states the discriminant: an actor-less membership change still carries meaning because the state change is the payload; for a receipt the reader *is* the payload. Emitting `sender: undefined` would be noise that looks like signal. Consequence worth documenting as a guarantee: **`sender` is always defined on an inbound iMessage read receipt** (still typed `User | undefined` because the framework-level `Message.sender` is).

**Compatibility.** Nothing breaks at the type level: `readSchema` has been in the `Content` union since it shipped, so exhaustive `switch (message.content.type)` already compiles. What changes at runtime is that `app.messages` starts yielding a content type existing loops have never seen — a deployed app whose `default:` arm replies to the user will now reply to read receipts. Ship as **12.5.0** with that stated prominently at the top of the release notes; if you'd rather treat any new inbound surface as breaking, 13.0.0 is the conservative call. Everything else is additive: one core export, one dead-until-Cloud webhook case, one `wrapNestedContent` arm that is inert on the outbound path.

---

## Testing

