## Docs

Mintlify `.mdx.vel` vellum templates, rendered centrally from `main` against the *released* package — so docs referencing new symbols must land in the same release as the code.

1. **`docs/content/read.mdx.vel`** (new — there is no `read` page today, even though outbound `read()` has shipped). Outbound half mirrors `docs/content/unsend.mdx.vel`; inbound half mirrors `docs/content/membership.mdx.vel:35-63`. Per-platform granularity table is already written in the JSDoc at `packages/core/src/content/read.ts:17-26`. The `## Inbound read receipts` section states: `sender` is the reader; `target` is the message *you* sent (`direction: "outbound"`, so `.edit()`/`.unsend()` work on it); `timestamp` is when it was read, not observed; **one message per reader in a group**; and `case "read":` on `app.messages` is always a receipt — outbound `read` is fire-and-forget and produces no Message, so no direction check is needed.
2. **`docs/nav.json`** — insert `"spectrum-ts/content/read"` after `content/unsend` (groups with the fire-and-forget signals).
3. **`docs/providers/imessage/messaging-features.mdx.vel`** — new `## Inbound read receipts` section after `## Inbound group events` (`:145-179`), mirroring its table + `<Warning>` pattern. The warning covers volume: one event per participant per message, and because `chats.markRead` marks *every* unread message, one chat-open can produce a burst — make sure your loop's `default:` arm doesn't reply to them. "Semantics to rely on" bullets: sender always present; timestamp is read time; aggregate by `target.id`; the agent's own `space.read(...)` never echoes; a restart replays receipts and may drop ones whose target can no longer be resolved.
4. **`docs/messages.mdx.vel:106`** — the `"read"` row is outbound-only framing ("mark the conversation read through the target message"). Rewrite for both directions. Extend the paragraph at `:110` (currently only group-management events) to name `read` as the other bidirectional inbound arm.
5. **`docs/providers/imessage.mdx.vel:126`** and **`docs/providers/imessage/connection-and-routing.mdx.vel:68`** — the capability row bundles "read receipts" meaning the *outbound* `space.read()`. Split it and add an inbound row.
6. **`docs/custom-events-and-lifecycle.mdx.vel:10,42`** uses read receipts as its example of the `events?` escape hatch — now the road not taken. Reword to "presence / delivery status" so it doesn't contradict the shipped design.

---

## Sequence

1. **Core plumbing** — `authoring.ts` export, `wrapNestedContent` arm, `receive-read.test.ts`, `content/read.test.ts`. *Gate: `packages/core` green, no existing test modified.*
2. **Extract the resolver** — `inbound.ts` add, `reactions.ts` delegate, one added reaction test. *Gate: all five existing reaction tests pass unmodified. If any needs editing, stop — the refactor changed behavior.*
3. **Mapping module** — `read-receipts.ts` + its 10 tests. *Gate: semantics fully pinned before touching the stream.*
4. **Wire the stream** — `isActorCurrentAccount` split, the `toMessageItem` branch, `stream.test.ts`. *Gate: the cursor-advance test is the release blocker.*
5. **Webhook parity** — `deserialize.ts` case + 2 tests. Independent; same PR or trailing.
6. **Docs** — must land in the same release as step 4.
7. **Release 12.5.0** via `scripts/release/bump-version.ts` (lockstep), with the behavior change at the top of the notes.

---

## Verification

```bash
bun install
bun run test:node && bun run test:bun   # both runners, per repo convention
bun x ultracite check
bun run build                            # typecheck through the metapackage
```

Targeted while iterating:

```bash
bun run --filter @spectrum-ts/imessage test -- read-receipts
bun run --filter @spectrum-ts/core test -- receive-read
```

End-to-end against a real line (the only way to settle `isFromMe` empirically):

1. Run `examples/basic` against a dedicated line with a `case "read":` arm that logs `sender.id`, `content.target.id`, `timestamp`.
2. Send a message from the agent; read it on the recipient device. Expect exactly one `read` message, `sender` = the recipient, `target.id` = the sent guid, `target.direction === "outbound"`.
3. Log `event.isFromMe` on the read branch during this run and record the observed value in the PR — it confirms (or refutes) the reasoning behind decision 3. Either value must produce the same behavior; if it doesn't, test #7 above is wrong.
4. Call `space.read(inboundMessage)` and confirm **no** `read` message echoes back.
5. Repeat in a group with 3+ participants: expect one message per reader, distinct ids, identical `target.id`.
6. Restart the process, then read an older agent message from the recipient device: confirm the receipt either resolves via `messages.get` or is dropped cleanly, with no stream stall.
```

